UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 7, Week 7, Lesson 2: Specialised cells', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Year 7 science develops specialised cells using scientific models, school-safe practical skills and evidence-based conclusions. State the question or idea, apply a clear method or explanation, record observations with units and link claims to evidence. Catalogue alignment uses SC4-LW-04; content-point mapping remains pending.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same science idea to a new object or material, then compare two pieces of evidence for strength.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nExplain one living-systems idea for specialised cells with a labelled diagram or key and one evidence sentence.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: accurate biology idea with a clear diagram/key link; avoids invented organelles beyond class models.\n\nWATCH FOR\nMixing everyday ''germs'' talk with accurate microbe roles, or drawing food-chain arrows the wrong way.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nIndependently complete a task on specialised cells: produce notes/diagrams/data as appropriate, include evidence-linked claims, follow safety rules, and write a five-sentence reflection with one next science skill goal.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one observation or step you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Science","learning_intention":"Year 7 science develops specialised cells using scientific models, school-safe practical skills and evidence-based conclusions. State the question or idea, apply a clear method or explanation, record observations with units and link claims to evidence. Catalogue alignment uses SC4-LW-04; content-point mapping remains pending.","lesson_number":2,"lesson_role":"Apply and analyse","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: safe everyday objects for observation"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["SC4-LW-04"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Independently complete a task on specialised cells: produce notes/diagrams/data as appropriate, include evidence-linked claims, follow safety rules, and write a five-sentence reflection with one next science skill goal. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: Strong answer: accurate biology idea with a clear diagram/key link; avoids invented organelles beyond class models. Reasoning: I used specialised cells by attending to evidence rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Mixing everyday ''germs'' talk with accurate microbe roles, or drawing food-chain arrows the wrong way. Correction: Strong answer: accurate biology idea with a clear diagram/key link; avoids invented organelles beyond class models. Why: the correction restores accurate use of specialised cells.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Mixing everyday ''germs'' talk with accurate microbe roles, or drawing food-chain arrows the wrong way. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Investigate or describe a fresh example (not the worked objects) that uses specialised cells. Record evidence and explain how it supports your claim.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one science misconception about specialised cells, correct it with evidence, and say why the correction works.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require a new example, named observations and a claim–evidence link. / Require misconception + corrected claim + evidence reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Strong answer: accurate biology idea with a clear diagram/key link; avoids invented organelles beyond class models. This matches the lesson skill for specialised cells.","options":["Mixing everyday ''germs'' talk with accurate microbe roles, or drawing food-chain arrows the wrong way.","Ignore the observation and invent a more exciting result.","Copy the model objects and call that independent work.","Strong answer: accurate biology idea with a clear diagram/key link; avoids invented organelles beyond class models."],"question":"Explain one living-systems idea for specialised cells with a labelled diagram or key and one evidence sentence. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Mixing everyday ''germs'' talk with accurate microbe roles, or drawing food-chain arrows the wrong way. That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["Mixing everyday ''germs'' talk with accurate microbe roles, or drawing food-chain arrows the wrong way.","Year 7 science develops specialised cells using scientific models, school-safe practical skills and evidence-based conclusions. State the question or idea,…","Strong answer: accurate biology idea with a clear diagram/key link; avoids invented organelles beyond class models.","Careful use of evidence with clear evidence."],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising specialised cells?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named evidence, not by copying the model.","options":["Treat a missing measurement as if it were recorded.","Apply the idea with new evidence and name the evidence used.","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence.","Choose randomly because any option could be right."],"question":"A learner is practising specialised cells. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Trustworthy science claims rest on observable evidence.","options":["Mixing everyday ''germs'' talk with accurate microbe roles, or drawing food-chain arrows the wrong way.","Use observable features or measurements as evidence, not appearance alone.","Decide from which object looks most exciting.","Copy a label from the lesson title with no observation."],"question":"New examples: yeast making bread rise, a spinning fan, copper wire, a tadpole. Which habit makes a scientific sort or claim trustworthy?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Science claims need named observations linked to the claim.","options":["A label copied from the title with no evidence.","Mixing everyday ''germs'' talk with accurate microbe roles, or drawing food-chain arrows the wrong way.","Named observation plus how it supports the claim.","A longer story with no observation."],"question":"Which record best supports a science claim about specialised cells?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Strong answer: accurate biology idea with a clear diagram/key link; avoids invented organelles beyond class models. applies the science idea with evidence.","options":["Strong answer: accurate biology idea with a clear diagram/key link; avoids invented organelles beyond class models.","Mixing everyday ''germs'' talk with accurate microbe roles, or drawing food-chain arrows the wrong way.","Ignore the observation and invent a more exciting result.","Copy the model objects and call that independent work."],"question":"Using this idea — Year 7 science develops specialised cells using scientific models, school-safe practical skills… — which choice is strongest?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Fair tests change one variable and control the rest.","options":["Change several conditions at once so something happens faster.","Stop early when you like the first result.","Ignore measurements and decide by which looks nicer.","Change one variable and keep the other conditions the same."],"question":"Which investigation habit keeps a comparison fair?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Transfer needs a new object or context with evidence.","options":["Invent a dramatic result with no observation.","Copy the model objects and call that independent work.","Test or describe a new object or context and cite fresh evidence.","Repeat the worked example objects unchanged."],"question":"Which next step best shows transfer of specialised cells?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require a new example, named observations and a claim–evidence link.","question":"Investigate or describe a fresh example (not the worked objects) that uses specialised cells. Record evidence and explain how it supports your claim.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: Strong answer: accurate biology idea with a clear diagram/key link; avoids invented organelles beyond class models. Reasoning: I used specialised cells by attending to evidence rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require misconception + corrected claim + evidence reason.","question":"Name one science misconception about specialised cells, correct it with evidence, and say why the correction works.","sample_answer":"Common error: Mixing everyday ''germs'' talk with accurate microbe roles, or drawing food-chain arrows the wrong way. Correction: Strong answer: accurate biology idea with a clear diagram/key link; avoids invented organelles beyond class models. Why: the correction restores accurate use of specialised cells.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s4-sci-y7-w07-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA Science and Technology alignment using catalogue outcome codes; content-point mapping remains pending.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S4","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise specialised cells.","Read the mission before you start observing or testing.","Keep the property or variable you are studying in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your evidence with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the observation or step that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore observations or fair tests.","Prefer accurate evidence over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your evidence and reason are both present.","Proofread labels, measurements and sequence of steps.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact observation or test step before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key observations.","Say what each observation shows about the idea.","A final label alone is weaker than recorded evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak claim with stronger evidence.","Change the claim or method, not only how neat the drawing looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh object or context that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original evidence and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new objects or data."]},"subject":"Living World","success_criteria":["I can use specialised cells with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify evidence and explain why it matters.","I can practise safety independently on a new prompt.","I can notice model needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Year 7 science develops specialised cells using scientific models, school-safe practical skills and evidence-based conclusions. State the question or idea, apply a clear method or explanation, record observations with units and link claims to evidence. Catalogue alignment uses…","options":["An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no evidence.","Year 7 science develops specialised cells using scientific models, school-safe practical skills and evidence-based conclusions. State the question or idea, apply a clear method or explanation, record observations with units and link claims to evidence. Catalogue alignment uses SC4-LW-04; content-point mapping remains pending.","A claim unrelated to the observation or test."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about specialised cells?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample for Specialised cells: relate structure to function (e.g. root hair cells increase surface area for water uptake) or place an organism in a food web with arrows showing energy flow.\n\nTRY FIRST\nExplain one living-systems idea for specialised cells with a labelled diagram or key and one evidence sentence.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: accurate biology idea with a clear diagram/key link; avoids invented organelles beyond class models.","explain":"Year 7 science develops specialised cells using scientific models, school-safe practical skills and evidence-based conclusions. State the question or idea, apply a clear method or explanation, record observations with units and link claims to evidence. Catalogue alignment uses SC4-LW-04; content-point mapping remains pending.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen or measured.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Mixing everyday ''germs'' talk with accurate microbe roles, or drawing food-chain arrows the wrong way. Correct the claim or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Specialised cells.","caption":"Link claim to evidence.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Idea</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">science concept</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Evidence</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">data/obs</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which observation matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: Example for Specialised cells: relate structure to function (e.g. root hair cells increase surface area for water uptake Then write your own evidence.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which observation matters most.\nJuno: Name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: Example for Specialised cells: relate structure to function (e.g. root hair cells increase surface area for water uptake Then write your own evidence."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Make the science idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim. Supported records follow the actual observations and purpose of the task.","options":["Make the science idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim.","A claim unrelated to the observation or test.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no evidence."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about specialised cells?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample for Specialised cells: relate structure to function (e.g. root hair cells increase surface area for water uptake) or place an organism in a food web with arrows showing energy flow. Look at each observation, source detail or example that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key observations, sources or steps from the model and say what each one shows.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful record names the evidence and the reason it supports the claim, not only a label.","explain":"Make the science idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen or measured.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without evidence does not show science thinking. Correct the claim or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Show the evidence","visual":[{"alt":"Science habits.","caption":"Models help explain.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Safety</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">school-safe</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Reflect</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">next skill</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific science error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim. Supported records follow the actual observations and purpose of the task.","options":["A guess with no evidence.","Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific science error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim.","A claim unrelated to the observation or test.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about specialised cells?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nMixing everyday ''germs'' talk with accurate microbe roles, or drawing food-chain arrows the wrong way. Here is a more productive model: Example for Specialised cells: relate structure to function (e.g. root hair cells increase surface area for water uptake) or place an organism in a food web with arrows showing energy flow.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against claim fails, then write a corrected claim with evidence.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: accurate biology idea with a clear diagram/key link; avoids invented organelles beyond class models.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific science error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen or measured.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without repairing the evidence does not fix the error. Mixing everyday ''germs'' talk with accurate microbe roles, or drawing food-chain arrows the wrong way. Correct the claim or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use the same science idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence. Supported records follow the actual observations and purpose of the task.","options":["Use the same science idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence.","A claim unrelated to the observation or test.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no evidence."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about specialised cells?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nYear 7 science develops specialised cells using scientific models, school-safe practical skills and evidence-based conclusions. State the question or idea, apply a clear method or explanation, record observations with units and link claims to evidence. Catalogue alignment uses SC4-LW-04; content-point mapping remains pending. Now choose a different example to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nInquire into or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short evidence-based reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same science idea as the model, but contain different evidence.","explain":"Use the same science idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen or measured.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new evidence. Correct the claim or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Transfer to a new investigation"}],"title":"Year 7, Week 7, Lesson 2: Specialised cells","unit_number":2,"unit_title":"Cells and classification","week":7,"week_in_unit":2,"word_c